pidly set writes project fields and refuses the format's:

  pidly set 1 title "a new title"   -> pidly: no field 'title' in the schema
  pidly set 3 needs ""              -> pidly: no field 'needs' in the schema

Which is correct as a rule — the schema declares project fields and lines 1,
2 and 3 belong to the format — but it leaves two ordinary operations with no
command at all:

  retitle                   sed -i '3s/.*/title: .../'
  add or drop a dependency  sed -i '2s/.*/needs: 24/'

Both are real ongoing work rather than corrections at creation time. new -n
wires dependencies only when the ticket is made, and a ticket that turns out
to be blocked later is the normal case, not the exception.

The danger is specific: a hand sed on line 2 or 3 has nothing checking what
it writes. A needs: naming a number that does not exist is a check finding
the author will not see until CI, and a botched line 3 stops being a title
line at all. A command validates before writing; sed does not.

Decide the surface: whether set learns the format's lines as a special case,
whether a separate command owns them, or whether needs gets its own verb
because adding and dropping one dependency is not the same operation as
replacing the line. Adding one dependency to a ticket that has three should
not require the caller to retype the other two.

Done when retitling and changing dependencies need no sed, a dependency on a
number that does not exist is refused at write time rather than found by
check, and the reference still documents the hand idiom beside the command.


Done 2026-09-18. set learns the format's lines, and needs gets a verb.

  pidly set 7 title "..."     line 3
  pidly set 7 needs "3 8"     line 2, replacing it
  pidly needs 7               what it needs, one a line
  pidly needs -a 24 7         add one, keeping the rest
  pidly needs -d 3 7          drop one

The rule the ticket worried about is intact: the schema declares what a
project wants, and lines 1 to 3 are what a ticket is. set does not pretend
they are schema fields — they are a case ahead of the schema lookup, with
their own validation — and the error for an undeclared name is unchanged.

What the command buys over sed, which is the whole argument for it: a needs
naming a ticket that does not exist is refused at write time, and so is a
ticket needing itself. Both were previously check findings that surface in CI,
after whoever wrote them has gone. The line is left untouched when the write
is refused.

claim is line 1 and set refuses it: it is written by claim, release and reap,
which know what it means. A caller who wants to clear one has reap.

needs -a exists because adding one dependency to a ticket that has three
should not mean retyping the other two — that retype is 50's hazard in
miniature. Dropping one that is not there is an error rather than a silent
no-op: the caller believed in a dependency that does not exist, and saying
nothing leaves them believing it.

set_line writes through the environment rather than awk -v, because awk -v
processes escape sequences and a title holding \t or \\ would arrive changed.
Tested.

That is not true of the existing set_field, which does use awk -v: a project
field value containing a backslash is silently corrupted today. Different
code path, so it is 51 rather than a quiet fix here.


One correction to the paragraph above, from finishing the work: there was
already a set_line, taking a whole line rather than a key and a value, and
the first version of this change defined a second one with the same name. The
later definition won, so claim, release and reap started passing three
arguments to a function expecting four and died under set -u. portable.sh
caught it — it is the suite that runs every command — and the fix is one
set_line, not two: it takes the whole line as it always did, and the callers
here build "title: ..." and "needs: ..." themselves.

Making that one escape-safe fixed the claim path too, which had the same
latent bug. It never showed because a claim is digits, dots, an @ and a
hostname, none of which awk -v rewrites.
